/**
 * @file unconnectedCircuits.cnl
 *   Test case for linNet.\n
 * This netlist file contains two unconnected circuits. They are nearly identical; it are
 * input followers using an op-amp with direct feedback from output to inverting input. The
 * only difference is an additional voltage source in the feedback of the second op-amp,
 * which results in an offset between in- and output voltage. Both circuits have a resistor
 * in parallel to the input voltage. Due to the infinite input impedance of the (ideal)
 * op-amp, this resistor becomes the input impedance of the circuit.\n
 *   The netlist exists in two variants. The other file unconnectedCircuits.noGnd.cnl has
 * the same circuit but node K01_gnd is named K01. This leads to an interesting and
 * important error message; the circuit can't be calculated.\n
 *   Further observations with this netlist:\n
 *   1) Including two unconnected circuits in a single netlist does not affect the
 * calculated results compared to placing each circuit in its own netlist file. However, it
 * does affect how the results are represented: the result of one circuit is expressed as a
 * function that also includes known currents and voltages from the other (unconnected)
 * circuit. Although these additional terms have a factor of zero, they are still shown in
 * the output.\n
 *   Conclusion: If there is no functional connection between circuits, it is preferable to
 * place them in separate netlist files.\n
 *   2) The feedback line of the second op-amp contains a voltage source (which could be
 * the model of a diode in the true circuit). This leads to a delta in the output voltage.
 * This is shown correctly in the result, which is: Uout2(s) = Uin2(s) + Udelta(s).\n
 *   3) The shortcut of the inputs produced by Rin1=Rin2=0 doesn't affect the result
 * calculation: linNet calculates with the symbols Rin1 and Rin2, no matter, which value
 * they might have. Moreover, the demanded result depends neither on Rin1 nor on Rin2 - the
 * resistors impact the input impedances but not the transfer functions - and so the
 * shortcut would not even hinder Octave from doing the numeric calculations.\n
 *   4) The numeric coupling of the resistor values is not a functional coupling of the
 * circuits. It remain two unconnected circuits.
 *
 * Copyright (C) 2013-2026 Peter Vranken (mailto:Peter_Vranken@Yahoo.de)
 *
 * This program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify it
 * under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by the
 * Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or (at your
 * option) any later version.
 *
 * This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but
 * WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
 * MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See the GNU
 * General Public License for more details.
 *
 * You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License along
 * with this program. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 */

/* An input follower with input impedance reduced to Rin1. */
U Uin1 K11 K01_gnd
R Rin1 K11 K01_gnd Rin1=0
OP OP1 K11 K21 K21
DEF Uout1 K21 K01_gnd

/* A second, unrelated, unconnected input follower with input impedance reduced to
   Rin2, which is 3 times Rin1. Note that the constant voltage source in the feedback line
   makes the output voltage follow the input voltage with a constant offset. */
U Uin2 K12 K02_gnd
R Rin2 K12 K22 Rin2=3*Rin1
OP OP2 K12 K22 K32
U Udelta K32 K22
DEF Uout2 K32 K02_gnd

RES Ambiguous Uout2